    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Ringbrothers Mesmerizes the SEMA Show Once Again, This Time With Billie, the 650-HP Pontiac GTO\nDDPAI Drops Their Latest Z100 Flagship Dash Cam and They've Outdone Themselves – Starts at $169\n13 photos\nPhoto: DDPAI\nWith SEMA underway as we speak, countless automotive OEM manufacturers are unveiling the peak of their lineups. One of those teams is DDPAI, a manufacturer of dashcams hitting the market with all they’ve got, and then some.\nEarlier this year, we tested a dashcam system from DDPAI. The Z90 Master was that unit, and after we put it to the test, we quickly realized that this brand means business. We even dropped it into a rally car to see if it can take the abuse, and it can.\nWell, building upon the success of their Z90 Master , DDPAI has unveiled the Z100 and its Pro and Master variations, which does all the Z90 brought to the table and then some, taking the brand further than ever before.\n{fullimg\nAlready, DDPAI mentions that ever since they hit the scene, they’ve served over 9 million users worldwide, so clearly, they’re doing something right. They have been around since 2013 and showing no signs of slowing down.\nDDPAI considers their latest Z100 as their new flagship model, meaning that it showcases the very best this brand has to offer, and we begin our exploration of the dashboard-mounted machine by taking a closer look at its design.\nFirst and foremost, the Z90 was a system that could be set up to operate on a 24/7 basis by drawing power from your vehicle’s battery in order to offer protection around the clock. However, this ability was only unlocked when using the 4G module that the Z90 included, a separate piece of hardware that you had to mount and wire.\n{fullimg\nWell, for the Z100, DDPAI has taken that module and incorporated it, among others, into the mounting bracket that you stick to your windshield in order to secure the Z100 in place. They did this to take some hassle out of the setup, which, from my own experience, I appreciate heavily; I had a difficult time mounting the module for the Z90.\nThat mounting bracket is what you see with a bit of a red strip lining the upper edge, which also incorporates the GPS module and wires for powering the Z100.\nAnother major difference we can see is that the Z100 also includes multiple lenses, toward the front, not just one, and it’s this feature that takes the Z100 to the next level. The benefits here are an ultra-wide angle of capture, and “telephoto views” of whatever the road ahead has to offer.\nThe wide-angle lens operates with a 4K resolution and is able to capture up to four lanes of traffic, and the telephoto lens runs at 2K. These specs are then coupled with a 4X+ optical zoon and 86 mm (equivalent) focal length. This allows the Z100 to also capture crystal-clear images of license plates from as far as 60 m (197 ft) away and an effective visibility of 100 m (328 ft).\n{fullimg\nAccording to a press release from DDPAI, the Z100 also has a few other tricks up its sleeve, one of which is an auto-adapting “dark to bright” aperture. At the front, the system switches between F1.0 and F2.0 without any manual adjusting required, ensuring that glare is limited during the day and at night, capturing as much light as possible, all in the name of ensuring that you and parties involved in an accident, know precisely what happened.\nKeep in mind, however, that there are rear cameras that are to be coupled with the Z100 too, one that checks out what’s happening behind your vehicle, and another in the cabin.\nKeep in mind that all of the cameras , including the master unit, include Sony’s Starvis 2 IMX678, 1/1.8-inch sensor with 8 MP, and just like the master, they’ll record at 4K. All three channels support HDR footage too, so exposure is balanced constantly, including glare and headlight reflections.\n{fullimg\nFurthermore, the ecosystem is completed with features like voice activation and control, along with 5G video transfers of footage to other devices, overheating protection, an integrated display on the backside of the Z100 Master, and if I’m not mistaken, infrared imaging inside the cabin at night.\nIf that last one is anything like the Z90, I can say that you’ll be surprised by how powerful the infrared is. When we tested the Z90, we could literally see our veins in the footage, which, if you ask me, is pretty mind-blowing. Let’s not forget about app controls.\nThe question is: How much cash will we need to get our hands on this piece of gear? According to our correspondence at DDPAI, in its least expensive variation, the Z100 rolls in with a price of $169 (€150). This is for the standard Z100 with a complete Z100 Master setup with three cameras and hardwire kit, rolling in at $539 (€480).\nIf you’re at SEMA during this period, DDPAI’s stall is located in the North Hall and sitting at #10861. They’re also carrying out reviews and tests on site too, so you can mess with the gear before you actually purchase it. Keep in mind that events such as these also entice clients to purchase products with an array of price cuts and deals to hook us, and that’s a win for anyone looking for a new dash cam; DDPAI confirms better offers at SEMA.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ncamera dash cam gear automotive Sony ddpai safety\nAbout the author: Cristian Curmei\nA bit of a nomad at heart (being born in Europe and raised in several places in the USA), Cristian is enamored with travel trailers, campers and bikes. He also tests and writes about urban means of transportation like scooters, mopeds and e-bikes (when he's not busy hosting our video stories and guides). Full profile\nUP NEXT The Tinytanic Is the World's Smallest Houseboat, Cute and Perfectly Functional",
